Kernel Development Practice Modules
=====================================

This directory contains a set of progressively more complex kernel modules
designed to help developers build familiarity with key Linux kernel
programming concepts.

Modules
-------

hello.c
    The simplest possible loadable module.  Demonstrates the module
    lifecycle (module_init / module_exit) and the pr_info() logging helper.

params.c
    Shows how to accept parameters at insmod time and expose them at
    runtime via /sys/module/<name>/parameters/.  Covers module_param(),
    MODULE_PARM_DESC(), and the int, charp, and bool parameter types.

chardev.c
    A minimal read/write character device.  Covers dynamic major
    allocation, struct cdev, class_create() / device_create(), struct
    file_operations, and safe user-space data transfer with
    copy_to_user() / copy_from_user().

procfs.c
    Creates a read-only /proc/sample_info entry using the seq_file
    interface (proc_create, proc_ops, single_open, seq_printf).

Build
-----

The modules are wired into the kernel build system.  Enable them via
menuconfig (Kernel hacking → Sample kernel code → Kernel development
practice modules) or set the corresponding options directly::

    CONFIG_SAMPLE_KERNEL_DEV=m
    CONFIG_SAMPLE_KERNEL_DEV_HELLO=m
    CONFIG_SAMPLE_KERNEL_DEV_PARAMS=m
    CONFIG_SAMPLE_KERNEL_DEV_CHARDEV=m
    CONFIG_SAMPLE_KERNEL_DEV_PROCFS=m

Then build with the normal kernel build process::

    make modules

Out-of-tree build
-----------------

Each .c file can also be built as a standalone out-of-tree module.
Create a local Makefile::

    # Makefile
    obj-m := hello.o

And invoke::

    make -C /lib/modules/$(shell uname -r)/build M=$(PWD) modules
    make -C /lib/modules/$(shell uname -r)/build M=$(PWD) clean

Usage
-----

After building::

    insmod hello.ko
    dmesg | tail

    insmod params.ko greeting="Hi" count=3
    cat /sys/module/params/parameters/greeting

    insmod chardev.ko
    echo "test" > /dev/chardev_sample
    cat /dev/chardev_sample

    insmod procfs.ko
    cat /proc/sample_info

    rmmod hello params chardev procfs
